Checking surviving authorship with `git blame`, and the results are in.

**Who owns the lines in HEAD (1,997 lines, 19 tracked files)**

| Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode | 810 | 41% |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 629 | 31% |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 95 | 5% |
| Jon Church | 72 | 4% |
| Sebastian Beltran | 67 | 3% |
| Ulises Gascón | 52 | 3% |
| Ignacio Mangas | 44 | 2% |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 30 | 2% |

Everyone else has fewer than 30 lines each.

**By area**
- `lib/index.js` (238 lines): Troy Goode 159 (67%), Douglas Christopher Wilson 22 (9%), Alejandro Caravaca Puchades 12, Raymond Feng 7. The core library is mostly Troy's original code.
- `test/`: Troy Goode 517, Douglas Christopher Wilson 374. Together they own about 85% of the test lines.

**Commits vs. surviving code**
- Douglas Christopher Wilson has the most commits (143 vs. Troy's 95). Troy still owns more surviving lines (810 vs. 629).

**Method and caveats**
- I ran `git blame` on every tracked file. I used `--ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs` so the 4-space and double-quote reformat commit (`62628c0`) doesn't take over ownership of every line.
- I also used `-w` to ignore whitespace changes and `-M -C` to follow moved and copied lines.
- Blame groups by author name, not by person. If someone committed under two names or emails, their lines are split across entries. I didn't check for that.
- Lines count equally here, whether code, docs, CI config or tests. Lines in `README.md` (277) and `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (220) count the same as lines in `lib/index.js`.
- A line's owner is whoever last substantively changed it. Someone who rewrote a line owns it now, even if the idea came from the previous author.

